---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const WEB = "server/src/iki_server/web";
const SETTINGS = "server/src/iki_server/settings.py";

const H = {
  short: "How the app reaches the browser, and what its policy allows",
  how: "The shell page and the static files",
  rules: "The content security policy and Trusted Types",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      Django answers every app path with one template, after the sign-in check: the CSRF token in a
      meta tag, module preloads and the entry script, and no data. Vite builds the app into Django's
      static files under content-hashed names, which are cached for good. The page's policy is
      strict: scripts and styles from Iki's own origin only, nothing inline, no <code>eval</code>,
      and Trusted Types required, with exactly one policy, which creates the app's own Web Workers
      and nothing else (<Adr n="0029" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      The shell is <Path p={`${WEB}/templates/web/app.html`} />, rendered by <Path
        p={`${WEB}/views.py`}
        sym="def app("
      />. It carries the CSRF token, the organization's name, the shared stylesheet, the
      entry module and the chunks worth fetching early. It's the same for everyone who's signed
      in. When the image has no build, the shell answers 503 with a page that says the web app is
      missing, rather than a blank page (<Path p={`${WEB}/frontend.py`} sym="def page_assets(" />).
      In development, a header from Vite's dev server switches the shell to Vite's modules; that
      setting is refused with HTTPS on, and the React refresh preamble is a file, since the policy
      allows no inline script.
    </p>
    <p>
      Vite builds into <code>/static/app/</code> (<Path p="web/app/vite.config.ts" sym='base: "/static/app/"' />).
      Django's manifest storage hashes and compresses the other static files but keeps Vite's own
      names, so chunks still import each other, and ServeStatic caches the app's files as immutable
      (<Path p={`${WEB}/frontend.py`} sym="class StaticFilesStorage" />, <Path
        p={`${WEB}/frontend.py`}
        sym="class ServeStaticMiddleware"
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="The page's content security policy" open>
        <p>Every server page, the shell among them, gets this policy:</p>
        <Excerpt file={SETTINGS} start="^SECURE_CSP = \{" />
        <p>
          Two directives depend on configuration. HTML responses also allow the identity
          provider's origin in <code>form-action</code>, because browsers apply it to the redirect
          after a sign-in form posts; and <code>connect-src</code> also allows the one origin
          object storage signs upload URLs for, since browsers upload files' parts straight there
          (<Path p={`${WEB}/csp.py`} />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Trusted Types, and the workers' policy">
        <p>
          Trusted Types are required for scripts, with exactly one policy, <code>iki-workers</code>.
          It allows only Iki's own worker scripts, from Iki's origin, named as Vite names them
          (<Path p="web/app/src/reading.ts" sym='createPolicy("iki-workers"' />). A worker runs under
          the policy of its script's response, not its page's, so worker scripts get a policy of their
          own that lets them load and run WebAssembly from Iki's origin and nothing else (<Path
            p={`${WEB}/static_headers.py`}
            sym="WORKER_CSP = ("
          />). An ESLint rule refuses <code>dangerouslySetInnerHTML</code>, which Trusted Types would
          refuse anyway (<Path p="eslint.config.js" sym="const RAW_HTML" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          Plugins' frames and Iki's own viewers have policies of their own: <PageLink
            to="Plugin frames"
            hash="rules"
          />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Violations are reported and tested">
        <p>
          Browsers post violations to <code>/csp-report/</code>, which is rate-limited per client and
          logs the directive and the blocked and document URLs without their query strings (<Path
            p={`${WEB}/views.py`}
            sym="def csp_report("
          />). The browser tests run against the production policy, with Trusted Types enforced, and
          fail any page that breaks a rule (<PageLink to="Browser tests" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Where the ADRs and the code differ">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <Adr n="0029" /> says the policy allows no Trusted Types policies (<code
              >trusted-types 'none'</code
            >); the code has one, for workers. Comments in <Path p="web/app/src/uploads.ts" /> and <Path
              p="web/app/README.md"
            /> still say workers aren't allowed.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={`${WEB}/views.py`} />: the shell and the violation reports</li>
        <li><Path p={`${WEB}/frontend.py`} />: the build's assets and how they're served</li>
        <li><Path p={`${WEB}/csp.py`} />, <Path p={`${WEB}/static_headers.py`} />: the policies</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0029" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0088" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
